475	// --- landing THIS cycle's PR when the arm could not (#649) --------------------
476	// The disposal above is correct and a full day late. On a member whose
477	// pull_request run is gated, EVERY cycle's arm fails, so every converge waits for
478	// the next cycle to land it — a standing ~24h offset between canon and that
479	// member's main, which reads as a mount that is permanently stale.
480	//
481	// The evidence that settles it does not take a day to arrive: the dispatched run
482	// this cycle just started finishes in seconds. So we wait for it, once, and merge
483	// on exactly the reasoning disposal uses. Same decision, made now.
484	//
485	// Bounded and cheap because it only runs where the arm ALREADY failed: a healthy
486	// member arms and returns without waiting at all. The bound is well inside
487	// `prework_timeout` (900s), and hitting it costs nothing — the PR is
488	// simply left for the next cycle, exactly as before this existed.
489	export const LAND_TIMEOUT_MS = 180_000;
490	export const LAND_POLL_MS = 5_000;
491	
492	// What one poll of the head sha's runs says to do: `merge`, `poll` again, or
493	// `give-up` and leave the PR standing.
494	//
495	// It NEVER closes. Disposal may close a PR because that PR is last cycle's and
496	// this cycle is about to re-cut it; here the PR IS this cycle's delivery, and
497	// closing it would throw away the converge that just ran. A red member therefore
498	// leaves its PR open for the next cycle to dispose of properly — the behaviour
499	// that existed before this, reached deliberately rather than by omission.
500	export function landAttempt({ delivery, runs, elapsedMs = 0, timeoutMs = LAND_TIMEOUT_MS }) {
501	  const disposition = pullDisposition({ delivery, runs });
502	  if (disposition === 'merge') return 'merge';
503	  if (disposition === 'wait') return elapsedMs >= timeoutMs ? 'give-up' : 'poll';
504	  return 'give-up';
505	}
